00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:23.950
Something I have been really
excited to ask you all about

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.830
is about the
community-building that you all

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:32.740
have done at Dow and about data
competitions and conferences.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.243
And I just posted on
LinkedIn earlier this week

00:05:36.243 --> 00:05:37.660
that a lot of
people have recently

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.990
been asking me about
how to get started

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:44.440
with their own internal
data conference.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.220
And I've been
asking for some tips

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.220
that I've learned from
others because I've never

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:49.885
done that myself.

00:05:49.885 --> 00:05:52.510
So I would love to just be able
to hear a little bit about some

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:56.150
of the events that
you lead, too.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.390
Amanda, maybe you can
kick off with the background

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.830
of the community and the
journey that we've been on?

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.320
AMANDA AHRENS: Yeah,
that sounds good.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:08.770
So we have an internal
data science community

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:13.030
that really has allowed us to
lay the foundation for ever

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:17.620
even being able to approach
a data science conference.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:21.190
And we did successfully have
a full data science conference

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.780
last year.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:26.140
So we call it the Insight
Scientist Community

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:29.410
and it was established in 2015.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.200
And I had the
privilege of helping

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.580
to launch it as one of the
managers for the community

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:39.010
at that point in time.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:45.580
And since then, it has grown
to over 500 members inside Dow.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.180
And of those, about
100 of those people

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.000
are full-time practitioners,
and the remaining

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.870
are interested parties, citizen
data scientists, people getting

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:58.480
into data science, learning.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.280
And we have a good breakdown
a big chunk, about 35%

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:09.920
from R&amp;amp;D. We have another
third from operations.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.530
And then a good chunk as well
from IT and supply chain,

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.390
and then the rest is scattered
across the other functions

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.100
and businesses
within the company.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.270
So throughout the years,
we've been able to do--

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.020
we started off smaller
with poster sessions.

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:31.300
We've kept up these
monthly technical meetings

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.360
where people can showcase--

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.730
just showcase what
they've been working on.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:42.280
So it's a more constant
feed of information

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.380
out to the community.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.680
But then we were able to
do our first data science

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.140
challenge in 2017.

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:53.500
And then we did
another one in 2019,

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.730
and then we did our last one as
part of the full Data Science

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:01.030
Conference in 2022.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:06.430
So it's been quite
a journey so far.

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:07.930
So that's a little
bit of background

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.300
on the foundation
for how we were

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.860
able to get this all together.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:19.290
I'm not as used to
having two leaders on here,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.840
so feel free to
just jump in when

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.270
you want if you have
something else to add, too,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.020
but I will also direct the
questions to both of you,

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:30.660
so whoever feels like answering.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.370
But I remember when
we were talking

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.700
a little bit about
the competition,

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.200
you mentioned that you had some
executives who are part of that

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:46.540
as well, and they were helping
judge some of the submissions.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.210
And I was just
curious, how did you

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:54.010
go about bringing this community
up to the executive level

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:57.175
as well and do you have any
tips for all of us to do that?

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:03.080
Yeah, so-- go ahead, Alix.

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:03.680
Yeah.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.220
We are very lucky
that we've had really

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.870
strong executive sponsorship
even from the beginning.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:15.260
So when Amanda was there at
the founding of the community,

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:18.937
I think we-- or the
sponsor, the manager

00:09:18.937 --> 00:09:21.020
that really said yes, I'm
going to put my people's

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.140
time into starting to do this,
I think reported directly

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.120
to our CIO or maybe two levels.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.200
So it was a very
close connection

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.390
to executive leadership
even from the beginning.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:37.660
And it was actually that
executive leader, our CIO,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:43.660
Melanie Kalmar, who I think
proposed the first event that

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.760
ended up being the
data science challenge.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.530
So we had had many events.

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:52.870
We did poster sessions, we
did seminars, career seminars

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:57.400
as well for our members, but
this was the first thing that

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.560
was really like--

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.050
I think the executive
leaders said,

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:07.270
how can we use this community
to start creating value

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:09.010
for the company?

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:14.320
I think-- I've got to believe
that's what originally

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:15.380
came to their minds.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:19.870
And so they suggested, why
don't you go figure out

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.820
how to do a hackathon?

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:26.020
And that word can mean a lot
of things to a lot of people.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:28.780
But after really
talking about it,

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:31.390
we settled on this
sort of Shark Tank--

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:37.150
like the TV show Shark Tank
style thing where groups

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.130
come up with a project
idea and pitch it

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.140
to this executive panel.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:49.090
So I don't know if, Amanda,
you were involved in--

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:52.870
at a step from getting that
executive sponsor, the one,

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:55.780
to getting a panel
of eight executives

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.680
in the first challenge?

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:06.310
Not so much the first
one, but in other areas,

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.850
I feel like what
really works is having

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:13.420
the executive that's already
on board sell it to the rest.

00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:19.990
Like, it doesn't work so much
if you're out there by yourself.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:23.380
If you can go along with
that executive that's already

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:27.910
on board and explain
the vision of why

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:30.760
you want to do a Data Science
Challenge or a Data Science

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.090
Conference and what's
in it for them,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:37.900
it was pretty easy to
get them to say yes,

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.720
because they felt like
it was an opportunity

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.120
that the other
executive was giving

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.990
them to really help
set data science

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:49.330
direction for the company.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:55.930
Because, I mean, in all three
Data Science Challenges,

00:11:55.930 --> 00:12:01.870
winning projects were
implemented and in some way.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.480
Even if they weren't
funded directly initially,

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.480
there was such high
interest by the executives

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:11.278
in moving forward with the idea.

00:12:11.278 --> 00:12:12.070
ALIX SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:12.570
All right. The

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.680
number one project
from last year

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:18.310
actually just won
an Edison Award,

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.840
which is a really big research
and development in STEM award.

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:26.020
And so that's like an external
recognition, too, of that.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:31.130
And that project, I mean,
pieces of it were happening,

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:34.210
but one of the things we try
to do with the competition

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:38.500
element of the event is
say, really, a lot of value

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:41.740
is created at the interface
of different functions

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.540
and different
organizations in a company

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.990
as big as Dow, which is
like 37,000 employees.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.350
And so one of the things
we encourage folks to do

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:55.870
is like network and get
out there and find people

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.480
in parts of the company that
you wouldn't normally talk to.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.250
And so that project
actually brought

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:07.870
three very small projects from
our toxicology department,

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:12.940
our molecular discovery research
area, and our supply chain area

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.310
together to make a
really powerful tool

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:20.840
for making safer and more
sustainable products.

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:26.440
So I think that one is like such
an awesome example of exactly

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:30.730
the vision of the event and to
get that external recognition

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:34.040
was really cool.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:35.120
That's amazing.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.050
It's really cool to hear how
you brought together people

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.940
from all different parts
of the organization,

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:43.438
too, who maybe would have
never worked together.

00:13:43.438 --> 00:13:44.230
ALIX SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:44.800
Yeah.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:51.250
I think a huge part of being
able to do that is making sure

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:55.040
that you set a theme that
resonates with the executives,

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.830
but also, the mid-level
managers because they have

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.380
to be supportive
of their employees

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.410
actually participating in
the Data Science Challenge,

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.500
because seeing how ours
was Shark Tank style,

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:12.520
it wasn't just a day
or two days or whatever

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:17.080
where people were working on a
problem with the same data set.

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:20.690
It was actually over a
longer period of time.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:23.230
So I think beginning
to end, the challenge

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.520
was more around two months.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:29.090
And so there was a lot
of time spent there,

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:33.820
so people had to feel like it
was worthwhile for their team

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:35.660
to be spending
their time up there.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:40.220
And so the first theme
was more general.

00:14:40.220 --> 00:14:45.610
It was like improving EBITDA.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:50.080
And then the second theme was
around employee experience

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:55.060
and customer experience,
which was extremely hot--

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:58.240
like top of mind, a
hot topic at that time.

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.060
I mean, it still is, but--

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.870
And then the last one
that we did in 2022

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:07.220
was all around sustainability.

00:15:07.220 --> 00:15:14.410
So it was on climate, especially
decarbonize and grow, water,

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:17.440
and circularity and
safer materials.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:20.230
And so that really
resonated with everybody.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:22.630
Everybody at Dow
is trying to prove

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.310
that they're
contributing to that,

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:26.860
and that united a lot
of people to come up

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:29.890
with some great ideas
cross-functionally.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:35.780
Did you come up with those
themes with the executives--

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:39.940
or like the original
executive sponsor?

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:44.130
So actually, it came--

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:47.410
the latest theme
was brought up to me

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:51.310
by one of the
technical principals

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:53.800
that we had at the
company who just thought

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:55.840
it would be a really
uniting theme,

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.310
and then we pitched
it to the executives

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:03.028
that we knew are already on
board and going to help us,

00:16:03.028 --> 00:16:04.570
and they were like,
yeah, absolutely,

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:05.740
let's go forward with it.

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:11.470
And we thought that
it was the right time.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:17.470
Dow seeks to be the most
sustainable material science

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:22.300
company in the world, and so
that's a very front and center

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.510
theme for the company, not just
the Data Science Challenge.

00:16:25.510 --> 00:16:29.500
And so it's pretty easy to
get executives to rally around

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:30.400
that.

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:32.590
it's like they were
already interested in it,

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:34.180
it was already on
their scorecard,

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:39.448
so might as well add this to
help them achieve their goals.

00:16:39.448 --> 00:16:40.990
ALIX SCHMIDT: But
finding themes that

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:44.020
are like enterprise-level
for the whole company

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:48.480
is really important, because one
of the things we were thinking

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.410
about is like, OK, let's make
sure the theme doesn't exclude

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:56.640
any one that has a certain type
of job that just doesn't touch

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:57.930
that area.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:02.580
So if we had a theme that was
more cost-effective materials,

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.464
R&amp;amp;D could work on
that, Manufacturing

00:17:04.464 --> 00:17:06.089
could work that,
probably Supply Chain,

00:17:06.089 --> 00:17:09.210
but you might have
people in Commercial

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:15.310
or Finance or Marketing that it
didn't really resonate as much.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:17.280
So one, we really
wanted to pay attention

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:22.210
to making sure the theme
could be touched by literally

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.490
every role in the
company so that we

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:26.230
weren't excluding anyone.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:31.300
So executive sponsors tend
to have goals at that level,

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:35.020
and so they did suggest
the first couple.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:41.080
The one that's really just
make more money, less exciting.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.990
So there is an
element that comes

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.960
from the technical
people's input on what they

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:48.920
want to work on.

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.310
So yeah, I think that
one's just really--

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:55.450
sometimes you just fall
into a magical happy place

00:17:55.450 --> 00:18:01.150
where everybody
aligns on what is fun.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:03.770
A great point.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:05.710
So I love talking
about community,

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.290
but we don't only have
to talk about community

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.060
and the competition here, too.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:13.270
I see, Libby, you had asked a
question a little bit earlier.

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:17.570
Do you want to jump
in here and ask that?

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:19.970
Yeah, it falls in line
with Katherine's question,

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:23.210
too, because Katherine was
asking about that same area

00:18:23.210 --> 00:18:27.050
with upskilling for
your tech stock.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.150
So she was asking you
about the curriculum

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.580
and how you tackled the
different use cases and stuff

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:33.110
and skill levels
that people have.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:38.040
And then my question was on the
end of getting leaders and ELT

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.360
people on the same page with
understanding what it actually

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.550
takes to deploy a model.

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:44.600
Yeah.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:49.250
So I think the different parts
of Dow have been on a journey

00:18:49.250 --> 00:18:53.330
with this, especially
as we start using

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:55.100
more of the cloud-based tools.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:59.870
We're a Microsoft
Azure company and we're

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:02.600
trying to use those things,
but of course, they're

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:07.200
changing and maturing
the technology every day.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.730
So I think there's been
definitely some journey

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:14.810
on understanding how to
make those things work

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:19.400
and how, especially in Dow,
there's the Microsoft support

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.970
page level of answer,
which is just technical,

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:25.410
but then in Dow,
there's the, OK, well,

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:27.650
how do I actually get
access to that thing

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:31.020
and who's the security approver
and all these other things.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:34.610
So that was pretty
much just a slog.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:36.990
Like we just had to like--

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:40.430
the first couple projects,
bless those people

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:41.945
because it was very painful.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:48.080
But they documented very well.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.750
So they documented the
internal work process

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:55.910
of how to make the external
technology work for us.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.010
So that's enterprise view.

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:01.850
My role is really
specifically in R&amp;amp;D.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:04.850
And in R&amp;amp;D, we have quite
a different situation

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:09.017
because it's research.

00:20:09.017 --> 00:20:10.600
And so you have this
research culture,

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:12.340
and if you haven't
worked in research,

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:16.870
it may be hard to
explain, or my explanation

00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:19.210
may fall one-dimensional.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.520
But you never know
what's going to happen.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:26.740
So you charter a project
and it's very difficult

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.200
to estimate the
next things that you

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:33.380
need because it could just
totally fail and whatever.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:41.020
And so that's the way R&amp;amp;D
project reviews and resourcing

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:42.500
is used to.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:48.210
And then you're working with all
these people from Information

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:52.830
Systems who have project
managers and they have--

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:56.130
we don't use Agile
for everything,

00:20:56.130 --> 00:21:00.870
but they're used to this
very formal Scrum scheduling

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:03.340
and you know exactly what
you want to implement,

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:06.090
and then you just have to do the
steps to get to the known end

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:07.330
product and whatever.

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.680
And those are just
like opposite things.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:19.530
So the situation that
we had was R&amp;amp;D managers

00:21:19.530 --> 00:21:26.730
sponsoring projects with a
researchy amount of funding,

00:21:26.730 --> 00:21:30.960
and then doing the research
part, and yay, that's good.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:35.310
But then they expected a
deployment out of the end

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:38.610
because that's what
you see when you

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:40.950
hear about AI and
machine learning

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:44.090
deployments in the world.

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.860
And so then that
was the same story.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:48.670
It's like, the first
few projects were just

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:50.170
really painful.

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:56.530
We had to come back to the
sponsors and say, I'm sorry,

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:58.870
this is going to take
like eight months longer

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.730
than you thought it
would and it's also

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:04.510
going to take three more
people, like a lot, lot more

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:07.910
than they had expected.

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:11.320
So now that we're on the
other side of that first batch

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:15.490
of really painful
projects, what I am trying

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:20.590
to do is really
capture what we learned

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:24.100
and work with those
people who now understand,

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.970
they're the ones who went
through the learning journey

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.800
and make sure we
can replicate that.

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:32.524
So it's things like--

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:36.960
literally-- so I'm preparing--

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.840
I'm going to be preparing
a mini-course called

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:41.610
Model Deployment 101.

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.410
It's just like literally,
what is a piece of software?

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.020
Like what is a
software architecture?

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:49.470
How do you know what you need?

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:55.220
And in my case, we have
a very powerful tool

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:59.210
which is using analogies
to chemical manufacturing.

00:22:59.210 --> 00:23:04.520
So everybody in R&amp;amp;D knows,
you make a little flask

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:06.750
of your chemical on the
lab, you're like yay,

00:23:06.750 --> 00:23:11.870
I got a new product, but
taking it from like this much

00:23:11.870 --> 00:23:16.610
on the lab bench to a
10,000-gallon reactor,

00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:21.320
that process is called scale-up,
they know that it's hard and it

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:23.570
doesn't work and it requires
all these specific types

00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:25.770
of engineer to make it happen.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.070
And so we're trying to say,
this is the exact same.

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:31.340
Just because you've done it in
your development environment

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:33.140
and this model
works really good,

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:35.390
there's still this
whole process to get

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:38.420
from that to a production
software solution.

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:40.940
And you can put up the
architecture diagram

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.940
and see, and be like,
look, these are like pipes.

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:45.500
Data has to flow through them.

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:49.430
Would you have a bench chemist
design your data pipes?

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:51.470
Like no, you wouldn't do that.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:54.530
That's why we have to have
this particular skill set

00:23:54.530 --> 00:23:56.840
on the team, and that's
why we have to have this,

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:57.740
and you know.

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:00.620
So that's been the most
powerful thing I've

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:04.400
heard a lot of feedback about.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:09.320
Just putting the tasks that
a deployment and development

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:15.920
team has to do into the
words of your client.

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:17.960
And so I'm trying to
get that documented,

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.030
get that in an easily
digestible form

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:22.190
so we can kind of get
the education out there

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:25.630
for the leaders.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:29.020
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I really
love that analogy, too.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:32.800
I know, Katherine you had
an original question, which

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:34.160
was tied into that, too.

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:38.207
So I want to make sure that
you jump in with any follow-up

