00:27:00.710 --> 00:27:03.590
So we'll have to--

00:27:03.590 --> 00:27:05.100
I'll have to think about it.

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:09.350
But I wonder if the
technical people that

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:11.750
are on the other side
of that conversation,

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:14.600
if they know exactly where
to go for that, then that

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:15.680
would be the first start.

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:16.638
KATHERINE GERTON: Yeah. There might be

00:27:16.638 --> 00:27:18.980
a way where you
could

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:22.370
put the trainings together
into a training flow,

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:23.690
like do one and the other.

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:24.740
ALIX SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:26.282
KATHERINE GERTON:
The people who have the answers can

00:27:26.282 --> 00:27:28.820
answer questions related to training
1 or 2

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:29.803
or something like

00:27:29.803 --> 00:27:31.970
that. But I don't know how
you set that up because I'm not

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:33.150
a web page developer.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:33.650
Yeah.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:37.820
We'll figure something out.

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:40.010
We're really-- I'm
really focused on--

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:41.939
because there's a very big--

00:27:44.657 --> 00:27:49.100
a very large amount of
learning between where

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:51.860
