00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:18.260
to need in one location
versus the other location

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:19.820
year over year?

00:29:19.820 --> 00:29:23.750
In people, in architecture,
in tools, all of that stuff.

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:27.500
Any particular ways
that you're successful?

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:28.040
Yeah.

00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:33.210
So I swear, we didn't pay
Alan to ask this question.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:34.500
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.740
The answer to that
is actually Posit.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:46.790
So in R&amp;amp;D, where Posit products
such as Workbench, Connect,

00:29:46.790 --> 00:29:49.880
Package Manager is
actually one of the things

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:52.520
that differentiates R&amp;amp;D
strategy from elsewhere

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:54.590
in the company that's
fully Azure-based

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:59.960
and does development in Azure
because it is so accessible.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:08.450
So the accessibility allows us
to have much more participation

00:30:08.450 --> 00:30:11.510
in data science from
all of our researchers.

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:15.290
And it's expected that 75%--

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:18.830
probably more, maybe
80 or 90% of things

00:30:18.830 --> 00:30:20.330
won't ever make
it to production,

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:21.930
and that's totally fine.

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:28.500
And so Posit is
excellent for that.

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:31.940
And then, of course, we
can use Connect to deploy.

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:35.570
And then if something
really scales big enough

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:37.760
and we have some kind
of technical reason

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:41.450
we need to move it to
Azure, then we can.

00:30:41.450 --> 00:30:43.340
So we think of it--
well, I think of it

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:47.420
as like an
accessibility pipeline.

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:51.200
We just want things to be
super easy in the beginning.

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:53.540
And then just make
sure that we have

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:57.200
governance in place to
recognize where we've

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:00.050
crossed some kind of
threshold where we do need

00:31:00.050 --> 00:31:01.790
improved reliability.

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:04.040
Maybe we actually need to
get an ML engineer involved

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:06.980
to make a hard-end
solution, or we

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:09.620
do need external connectivity.

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:13.160
So it is our policy in
Dow because our Posit

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:17.348
server is on-prem, we don't let
anybody outside of Dow into it.

00:31:17.348 --> 00:31:19.890
So if you have an app that you
want to release to a customer,

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:21.390
we have to do
something different.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:28.070
So it's on me to figure out
those governance thresholds

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:34.880
of when we need to do something
a little less accessible,

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:37.160
but that's how we
approach the agility,

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:40.820
is just making it super
easy to get started.

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:45.030
That's great, thank you.

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:48.700
And you didn't seed
that question to

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.700
me, but we're thinking
about exactly the same thing.

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:54.020
How do we keep an amount
of agility at one level

00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:56.090
while-- we're figuring
out a whole enterprise

00:31:56.090 --> 00:31:57.800
Azure stack now.

00:31:57.800 --> 00:32:02.420
So like I'm taking this to my
enterprise architecture folks

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:06.310
to say like, look,
this can work.

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:06.970
Yeah.

00:32:06.970 --> 00:32:11.560
I'll just add, too,
from like the rest

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:13.310
of Dow kind of experience.

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:16.990
So Dow has digital
centers which are

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:19.540
responsible for
digital innovation.

00:32:19.540 --> 00:32:22.630
There's information
research embedded in R&amp;amp;D,

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:28.120
and there's one for like
a digital marketplace that

00:32:28.120 --> 00:32:31.640
are more on the commercial side,
supply chain side, operations

00:32:31.640 --> 00:32:32.140
side.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.680
Anyways, I feel
like what's happened

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:39.160
is the original plan
was that there would

00:32:39.160 --> 00:32:41.830
be a sister organization
that would partner

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:45.280
to help support longer-term.

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:49.420
And we're still working toward
that and laying the groundwork,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:54.760
identifying skills needed that
aren't necessary necessarily

00:32:54.760 --> 00:32:56.860
in those service
organizations yet,

00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:59.080
and so we've been
building that over time.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:03.520
And then in the meantime,
those digital centers

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:10.450
are also working to support
those really impactful

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:13.420
new applications and
models and whatnot that

00:33:13.420 --> 00:33:17.290
have been built so that people
aren't experiencing them

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:19.720
for a short period of time
during a proof of concept

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.210
or a pilot and then
them going away.

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:26.170
So I will say we have some
growing pains that we're

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:31.270
working through
still, but certainly

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:34.570
making a lot of progress even
over the last five years.

00:33:37.190 --> 00:33:38.300
Great, thank you.

00:33:38.300 --> 00:33:41.480
So much of that is really
resonant and remarkable

00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:44.300
how similar some
of our thinking is,

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:48.830
but how we still have
lots of problems to solve.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:53.450
And yeah, it's great to hear
the way that you're coming at it

00:33:53.450 --> 00:33:55.580
and go, OK, we're not
completely out in the wild

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.980
and what we're thinking
about is great.

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.890
Thank you, I appreciate it.

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:02.130
Love it.

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:05.440
Abigail, I see you have
asked a great question

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:08.560
in the chat that's sparking a
lot of conversation there, too.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:09.972
Do you want to jump in next?

00:34:09.972 --> 00:34:10.889
ABIGAL HADDAD: Thanks.

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:13.830
So this is the only thing
we've run into when hiring.

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:17.960
How do you figure out this is a
skill that this position needs

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:19.710
to have coming in
versus this is something

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.429
that we can train people up in?

00:34:22.429 --> 00:34:24.449
Woo.

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:25.800
That's a hard question.

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:32.610
[LAUGHS] I think the only answer
that I have for that question I

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:40.380
think we really know is at
this point for, I would say,

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:43.389
our materials and
process-related positions--

00:34:43.389 --> 00:34:48.120
so in R&amp;amp;D and Manufacturing,
we have found that it is much

00:34:48.120 --> 00:34:52.800
easier to hire people with
degrees in that subject

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:53.380
matter--

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:55.230
so typically
chemical engineering.

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:59.400
And then build up the data
science skill from there

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:02.100
rather than hire a
computer scientist

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.290
and teach them enough
chemical engineering

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:07.470
to have those really powerful
conversations that you need

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:12.120
to have in data science
just because, I guess,

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:17.940
the engineers already have some
pretty deep background in math

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:20.310
and data.

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:22.990
But yeah, the domain expertise,
it's really important.

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:26.220
So I think that's the only
thing we really for sure,

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:30.510
is we want to start from
the domain expertise

00:35:30.510 --> 00:35:34.650
that we'll need for that role
and then add on digital skills

00:35:34.650 --> 00:35:37.260
from there.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:40.020
But I think it really is
different for different parts

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:47.600
of the project team in terms
of the DevOps engineering

00:35:47.600 --> 00:35:49.340
and that kind of stuff, is--

00:35:49.340 --> 00:35:51.500
or ML engineering.

00:35:51.500 --> 00:35:54.780
They need a lot less of
the domain expertise.

00:35:54.780 --> 00:36:00.050
So we haven't exactly
figured out, I would say,

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:05.750
the particular the best way to
hire for those positions yet,

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:09.500
other than that since the
tech-- a lot of the technology

00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:12.650
and especially the Azure
particular microservices

00:36:12.650 --> 00:36:16.760
and all that different stuff
is so new that we do recognize

00:36:16.760 --> 00:36:20.540
that there's nobody that's
got 10 years of experience

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:24.080
in Azure Data
Factory, for example.

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:26.780
So that gives you a
little bit of a relief

00:36:26.780 --> 00:36:31.430
knowing that everybody is in the
same boat and training people

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:34.115
and upskilling people right now.

00:36:34.115 --> 00:36:35.740
AMANDA AHRENS: Yeah,
I think it depends

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:39.470
on the different areas of Dow,
too, that you're looking at.

00:36:39.470 --> 00:36:42.190
So like if we--

00:36:42.190 --> 00:36:47.710
well, we share resumes across
the data science teams in Dow,

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:49.120
too.

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:52.120
Like our Common
Metrics Statistics

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:54.670
in Ag Group found
somebody that maybe

00:36:54.670 --> 00:37:01.990
doesn't have a robust process
chemistry background, then--

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:07.390
and maybe they're fitting for
a group like our Core Advanced

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:12.370
Analytics Team in IT that is
working more on supply chain

00:37:12.370 --> 00:37:17.230
and commercial
logistics challenges,

00:37:17.230 --> 00:37:19.700
then we can send that
over and be like,

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:22.300
look out for this person,
they have a really robust

00:37:22.300 --> 00:37:22.970
background.

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:26.600
So it's like-- it depends.

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:28.600
But I will say,
too, we realize--

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:35.880
I mean, ideally you would have
both the industry experience

00:37:35.880 --> 00:37:41.200
and background, as well as
some exposure to data science

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:42.760
or other digital skills.

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:46.420
And so Dow is
investing in programs

00:37:46.420 --> 00:37:47.515
that help provide that.

00:37:47.515 --> 00:37:52.480
Like Alix already mentioned,
the intern program

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:57.520
with interns starting that
have more of background--

00:37:57.520 --> 00:38:02.350
like they're pursuing PhDs
and chemistry or chemical

00:38:02.350 --> 00:38:04.660
engineering or
whatever, but then also

00:38:04.660 --> 00:38:09.310
they have some experience
in data science,

00:38:09.310 --> 00:38:11.440
and so we want to give
them more experience

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:15.520
at data science and those
digital opportunities.

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:19.900
Also, I am helping
to plan a program.

00:38:19.900 --> 00:38:22.780
It's called DAISE, Data
Analytics for Science Immersion

00:38:22.780 --> 00:38:25.510
Experience, kind of a mouthful.

00:38:25.510 --> 00:38:28.210
But it's actually
targeted undergrads

00:38:28.210 --> 00:38:30.940
that are studying STEM.

00:38:30.940 --> 00:38:35.140
And these are undergrads from
historically Black colleges

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:37.420
and universities mostly.

00:38:37.420 --> 00:38:42.730
And we are giving them
exposure to data sciences

00:38:42.730 --> 00:38:45.340
as a partnership with
Carnegie Mellon and Accenture.

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:47.960
And so they're going to be at
Carnegie Mellon for a week.

00:38:47.960 --> 00:38:50.590
They'll be learning
some of the--

00:38:50.590 --> 00:38:53.140
being exposed to some
of the lecture material

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:56.050
for a master's in
science program--

00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:58.123
master's and data science
program that they have.

00:38:58.123 --> 00:39:00.040
And then they're actually
going to come to Dow

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:04.300
and be able to see a poster
session where they're exposed

00:39:04.300 --> 00:39:09.310
to data science
projects at Dow and get

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:13.365
exposed to technical principles
and data science fellows

00:39:13.365 --> 00:39:15.740
and hear about their career
paths and that kind of thing.

00:39:15.740 --> 00:39:20.050
So we're trying to also
foster the skills that we

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:23.920
need to hire in the
future because we

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:26.350
know it is a
challenge right now,

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:28.010
it's a lot of one or the other.

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:30.340
So how can we create
those opportunities

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:34.290
where somebody would
already come in with both?

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:37.530
I will add one other thing.

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:39.540
You mentioned, Amanda,
it's a really good point

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:44.730
that we're well-connected
within our community enough

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:47.070
that if somebody
gets a resume that

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:50.490
would be a great add to
Dow but not their group,

00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:52.560
we know who to send it to.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:56.070
And I would say that's probably
mostly because of the Insight

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:57.450
Scientist Community.

00:39:57.450 --> 00:40:00.240
So the purpose of the
Insight Scientist Community

00:40:00.240 --> 00:40:04.500
is like to make sure that we
don't get too isolated in each

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:09.030
of our functions and that we're
sharing learnings because data

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:12.610
science does cross all domains.

00:40:12.610 --> 00:40:14.940
And so when we put
together-- just

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:18.180
to tie back to our
starting discussion.

00:40:18.180 --> 00:40:19.800
When we put together
the steering

00:40:19.800 --> 00:40:24.180
team of that community, which
we really only formalized,

00:40:24.180 --> 00:40:29.830
I don't know, maybe four
years ago now, we have--

00:40:29.830 --> 00:40:32.460
we make sure to have
one person from each

00:40:32.460 --> 00:40:37.410
of the major functions that
represent the community.

00:40:37.410 --> 00:40:43.320
And it's usually like somebody
pretty senior, mid-career,

00:40:43.320 --> 00:40:46.870
which is senior
for data science.

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:52.290
And that's very intentional.

00:40:52.290 --> 00:40:55.130
And so that that's a
place where we can always

00:40:55.130 --> 00:40:57.800
go to for that
cross-functional thing.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:00.650
Like even if I was in
the community but not

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:03.747
well-connected, I would know
I can send it to my R&amp;amp;D person

00:41:03.747 --> 00:41:05.330
and they'll circulate
it in that group

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:06.840
and it'll get to
the right place.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:12.620
So it does end up being a really
nice vehicle for communication

00:41:12.620 --> 00:41:13.340
in that way.

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:19.270
Is that a Teams
group or something,

00:41:19.270 --> 00:41:21.670
that you're all like in
constant communication on?

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:23.210
Or how is everybody--

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:23.710
Yeah.

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:31.090
I mean, we existed before we
had Microsoft Teams at Dow,

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:33.490
but I think--

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:37.690
I was actually the chair of that
steering team for two years.

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:40.100
I ended at the end
of last year, hooray!

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:46.360
And so we have monthly
meetings just to make sure

00:41:46.360 --> 00:41:48.310
we're making progress
on community goals.

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:50.740
So we did have
community-level goals

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:55.090
around providing training or
providing network opportunities

00:41:55.090 --> 00:42:00.560
or creating a sense of
belonging, like community.

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:02.890
And so monthly
meetings, and then yeah,

00:42:02.890 --> 00:42:06.730
we use now Microsoft
Teams, so chat and document

00:42:06.730 --> 00:42:11.500
storage and things like that all
in one place for coordinating.

00:42:11.500 --> 00:42:13.480
Yeah, just like
quick things that

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:17.670
come up in between
the monthly meetings.

00:42:17.670 --> 00:42:19.050
Something I thought--

00:42:19.050 --> 00:42:21.180
maybe I'm misremembering
this, but I

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:24.570
think we talked about you have
these cool like coffee hour

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:27.720
things that your team
does on Fridays where

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:29.910
you get to meet different
people from other teams.

00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:35.670
That is that part of the
Data Insights Community?

00:42:35.670 --> 00:42:38.290
d have a couple of
different things like that.

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:43.530
So we have technical seminars
for the full Insight Scientist

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:45.480
Community, but then we have--

00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:48.720
what we wanted to do
was enable our members

00:42:48.720 --> 00:42:54.120
to find any type of close-knit
community they wanted to.

00:42:54.120 --> 00:42:57.540
So you are allowed to make a
subchapter of the community

00:42:57.540 --> 00:43:00.730
as long as you have enough
interested people to do that.

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:03.160
And so we started
with local chapters.

00:43:03.160 --> 00:43:04.800
So we have certain sites.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.960
There's one in
Michigan, one in Texas,

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:11.940
there's one in the Netherlands,
and one in Shanghai.

00:43:11.940 --> 00:43:14.730
For where there's sort of a
high concentration of people,

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:16.680
they can have local
stuff, and then they

00:43:16.680 --> 00:43:19.750
have basically full autonomy
over what that group does.

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:22.950
So the Midland one, we pretty
much just have happy hours.

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:27.360
Other ones have book clubs
or presentations or things

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:29.112
like that.

00:43:29.112 --> 00:43:30.570
So that's one of
the things that we

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:34.110
do to get to know each other,
yeah, more on a personal level.

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:38.040
I know a couple different
groups within the company

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:43.680
have informal
technical chats right,

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:49.000
almost like a peer review
kind of thing to help people

00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:52.440
keep in touch with what
others are learning,

00:43:52.440 --> 00:43:56.770
but also get to know
each other a little bit.

00:43:56.770 --> 00:43:57.270
Yeah.

00:43:57.270 --> 00:43:59.880
So we really try to--

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:03.240
we're not like the end all,
be all of community planning.

00:44:03.240 --> 00:44:06.120
We try to create a
platform where people

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:07.800
can use our distribution
list if they

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:09.660
want to get a hold
of everyone, they

00:44:09.660 --> 00:44:11.850
can create another channel
in the Microsoft Teams

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:15.270
if they want to
make a subchapter.

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:18.390
Yeah, just be a
platform for folks

00:44:18.390 --> 00:44:21.975
to create whatever kinds of
groups they need to create.

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:25.280
Thank you.

00:44:25.280 --> 00:44:27.200
There was an anonymous
question a little bit

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:31.400
earlier that was, how do
you regain trust and rebuild

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:34.910
reputations after failing
to meet expectations,

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:36.925
whether time and budget?

00:44:36.925 --> 00:44:38.300
If you have
experience with this,

00:44:38.300 --> 00:44:41.450
and especially when dealing
with a difficult sponsor?

00:44:44.510 --> 00:44:46.220
Yeah.

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:48.090
That's a really,
really good question.

00:44:48.090 --> 00:44:51.170
I'll say, we definitely
understand exactly what

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:51.770
you're asking.

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.440
I'm trying to think
of a case where

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:01.920
it's actually the
person who we've

00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:03.600
gotten to come back around.

00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:06.780
I don't know, Amanda or James,
if you have anything in mind.

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:11.400
I actually don't know of
a single individual that

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:15.000
maybe has gotten burned by
some of the early learning

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:18.390
processes we were doing and then
we've gotten to come around.

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:23.880
We're maybe not there
yet mostly it's--

00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:29.050
organizationally, we do have
such senior leadership that's

00:45:29.050 --> 00:45:32.430
saying like, hey, this
stuff is really important,

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:37.150
we have to start
learning how to do it.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:44.930
So we do have senior leadership
behind us in terms of saying,

00:45:44.930 --> 00:45:48.920
OK, look, we have it out
there, this didn't go well,

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:50.650
but we need to try again.

00:45:50.650 --> 00:45:54.220
Like we have to try again,
m we don't have a choice.

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:57.250
But usually the try-again
is with somebody different.

00:45:57.250 --> 00:46:01.660
Just by coincidence just because
there's a lot of employees.

00:46:01.660 --> 00:46:05.540
So yeah, I don't know.

00:46:05.540 --> 00:46:08.570
Personally for just me
and how I run my projects,

00:46:08.570 --> 00:46:11.360
I just am transparent.

00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:14.360
I can just say like,
yeah, that was awful.

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:19.315
Like it was not fun for anybody,
we didn't expect such whatever,

00:46:19.315 --> 00:46:20.660
whatever, whatever.

00:46:20.660 --> 00:46:22.820
That's the only way.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:25.520
You can't cover it up, then
they're not going to trust you.

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:28.490
I think, yeah, transparency
and authenticity

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:31.100
is the only way to trust.

00:46:31.100 --> 00:46:31.970
Yeah.

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:34.580
I think-- I can't
think of an example

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:37.070
where I regain
somebody's trust, but I

00:46:37.070 --> 00:46:38.660
have had learnings from it.

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:44.720
So I explain, because I feel
like typical IT projects,

00:46:44.720 --> 00:46:48.920
because I've spent a good chunk
of my career at Dow in IT,

00:46:48.920 --> 00:46:54.020
typical IT projects, like the
only definition of success

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:57.830
is delivery of the
product that you were

00:46:57.830 --> 00:46:59.930
supposed to go out and deliver.

00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:04.280
Whereas like in R&amp;amp;D, that's
not necessarily the case.

00:47:04.280 --> 00:47:07.490
It's like, if you kill
a project quickly,

00:47:07.490 --> 00:47:11.340
that can still be
seen as a success.

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:15.560
And so I've been trying to
use that kind of analogy

00:47:15.560 --> 00:47:18.470
where it's like, hey, this
is-- we are really genuinely

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:21.270
trying to prove a concept here.

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:26.160
So if we execute this
project and find, hey,

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:28.250
this is absolutely
not going to work,

00:47:28.250 --> 00:47:30.420
this approach that we
were hoping would work,

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:32.270
that is actually
still a success.

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:34.550
And making sure
that the sponsors

00:47:34.550 --> 00:47:37.940
are clear on what that
definition of success

00:47:37.940 --> 00:47:39.320
is from the beginning.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:42.650
Because there are projects that
are like, OK, we've proved it,

00:47:42.650 --> 00:47:44.630
this is really more
we're going into a pilot

00:47:44.630 --> 00:47:46.490
or going into an
implementation, and we

00:47:46.490 --> 00:47:48.200
do expect delivery at the end.

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:50.310
That is the only
definition of success.

00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:52.760
So obviously if we
don't achieve that,

00:47:52.760 --> 00:47:55.880
then that would be
not good and they

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:57.270
would have problems with that.

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:59.960
So I feel like just
explaining that difference

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:04.460
and where project actually
is, and the, I guess,

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:08.150
realm of definition
of success is.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:13.000
Thank you both.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:16.150
I know-- we have about
10 minutes left here,

00:48:16.150 --> 00:48:17.610
so I want to make
sure that I don't

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:20.880
miss any questions that maybe
we're earlier in the chat, too.

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:24.000
So feel free to raise your
hand here on Zoom if you

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:26.770
have other questions, too.

00:48:26.770 --> 00:48:28.890
But something I wanted
to ask you both is,

00:48:28.890 --> 00:48:32.160
if you were to start
this process of training

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:34.870
and onboarding new data
scientists over again,

00:48:34.870 --> 00:48:37.980
is there anything that
you would do differently?

00:48:37.980 --> 00:48:40.320
Like having the experience
that you have now.

00:48:50.880 --> 00:48:53.370
I don't know that there is.

00:48:53.370 --> 00:48:57.930
I think-- it's really
hard to separate

00:48:57.930 --> 00:49:01.890
what our specific
people have done

00:49:01.890 --> 00:49:09.750
and what a data scientist job
looks like now versus five

00:49:09.750 --> 00:49:15.000
years ago from how the industry
as a whole has changed.

00:49:15.000 --> 00:49:18.420
Like if I knew we were going to
be here five years ago, I could

00:49:18.420 --> 00:49:19.980
have said, oh
yeah, we would have

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:23.820
done a lot more software
development, best practice

00:49:23.820 --> 00:49:30.080
training, and coding skills,
and some of that other stuff.

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:32.660
But it's not like--

00:49:32.660 --> 00:49:36.300
I don't know, I just think
just things have changed.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:40.820
And so we will change
and I just can't

00:49:40.820 --> 00:49:44.420
imagine what I would
have done knowing

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:45.890
what the future would hold.

00:49:45.890 --> 00:49:47.285
Yeah.

00:49:47.285 --> 00:49:48.160
That's a non-answer--

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:49.390
ALIX SCHMIDT: --for you.

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:55.870
Yeah, I can't think of
anything in particular.

00:49:55.870 --> 00:50:02.590
I will say, I like listening to
the new people's perspectives

00:50:02.590 --> 00:50:04.960
because--

00:50:04.960 --> 00:50:11.260
well, since I've been at Dow
for almost eight years now,

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:13.930
there's been pretty
drastic improvement.

00:50:13.930 --> 00:50:16.820
I'm not saying we
figured everything out,

00:50:16.820 --> 00:50:18.700
we don't have a long
way to go still.

00:50:18.700 --> 00:50:20.830
We do have a long
way to go still.

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:26.650
But I feel like sometimes
I'm more rosy about some

00:50:26.650 --> 00:50:30.460
of the progress, whereas
like somebody new could

00:50:30.460 --> 00:50:32.200
be like extremely frustrated.

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:35.470
Like, why can't I do this?

00:50:35.470 --> 00:50:39.200
Why am I having these access
issues and that kind of thing.

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:46.750
So it's like you're taking
note of what they have to say.

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:49.450
It's really critical
because, I mean,

00:50:49.450 --> 00:50:51.350
we would like to
retain them as well.

00:50:51.350 --> 00:50:53.830
So making sure that
their training experience

00:50:53.830 --> 00:50:57.640
is going well even
though we've been

00:50:57.640 --> 00:51:00.850
around to see an evolution.

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:02.260
Yeah.

00:51:02.260 --> 00:51:05.530
Actually, I was thinking
about that with Posit

00:51:05.530 --> 00:51:07.930
as well, because
when I started And.

00:51:07.930 --> 00:51:12.730
We were 60 people, it's also--
like an onboarding experience

00:51:12.730 --> 00:51:15.550
is going to be a lot different
than from when we have,

00:51:15.550 --> 00:51:19.360
I don't know, 300 and
something or 400 people

00:51:19.360 --> 00:51:22.750
and realizing that
people will have

00:51:22.750 --> 00:51:28.090
different expectations depending
on that company size, too.

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:31.240
Libby, I think you had asked
a question a few minutes ago.

00:51:31.240 --> 00:51:33.820
Do you want to jump in here?

00:51:33.820 --> 00:51:34.630
Yeah.

00:51:34.630 --> 00:51:37.130
And it was kind of along the
lines of what Amanda is talking about where people

00:51:37.130 --> 00:51:39.550
come in and they have
expectations

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:42.670
that they've brought with them.

00:51:42.670 --> 00:51:44.890
I have seen this happen
with a lot of people

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:46.160
and in a lot of organizations.

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:49.060
So I'm curious how
Dow handles it,

00:51:49.060 --> 00:51:51.670
when you upskill a
bunch of employees

00:51:51.670 --> 00:51:54.550
into better data skills
or a better data stack

00:51:54.550 --> 00:51:56.920
or whatever it is,
and then they are not

00:51:56.920 --> 00:51:59.470
able to actually use those
skills in their day-to-day job.

00:51:59.470 --> 00:52:01.810
I've seen it sow a lot
of dissent in people.

00:52:01.810 --> 00:52:04.060
They're like, OK, you want
me to learn all this stuff,

00:52:04.060 --> 00:52:06.250
but you've given me nowhere
to apply all of this,

00:52:06.250 --> 00:52:08.920
or I can't use that tool that
you taught me how to use,

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:09.860
whatever it is.

00:52:09.860 --> 00:52:11.890
So how do you guys handle that?

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:14.480
Or is it not an issue for now?

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:20.430
Sorry, million-dollar question.

00:52:20.430 --> 00:52:24.070
I'm letting Amanda go first.

00:52:24.070 --> 00:52:27.500
I mean, I've definitely
seen it be an issue at Dow,

00:52:27.500 --> 00:52:32.060
like when people go through
more generic outdoor service

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:36.510
training and then we
analyzed the environment,

00:52:36.510 --> 00:52:39.080
so then they can't do
things exactly how they

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:42.780
learned in the training, so
that can be really frustrating.

00:52:42.780 --> 00:52:46.220
And so we're still working
through combating that.

00:52:46.220 --> 00:52:49.250
But I think as far as
getting-- like giving people

00:52:49.250 --> 00:52:51.350
access to other tools,
I haven't seen so much.

00:52:51.350 --> 00:52:53.480
Like people want to go
do a JMP training, cool,

00:52:53.480 --> 00:52:55.855
then they'll have access to
JMP or [? SIMPER ?] [? ROW ?] or

00:52:55.855 --> 00:52:58.070
whatever it is.

00:52:58.070 --> 00:53:02.070
But I've definitely seen that.

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:06.650
And so from an Insight
Scientist Community perspective,

00:53:06.650 --> 00:53:12.380
we definitely
raised those issues

00:53:12.380 --> 00:53:16.190
to leadership that could
actually help us take action.

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:20.900
We got leadership to
help us talk to security

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:24.410
about the issues that were
preventing people from having

00:53:24.410 --> 00:53:27.470
a similar experience.

00:53:27.470 --> 00:53:32.330
I know Alix, James,
and I definitely

00:53:32.330 --> 00:53:36.030
are on many calls in the past
about those kinds of things.

00:53:36.030 --> 00:53:41.630
And so I think we still leverage
the insight scientist community

00:53:41.630 --> 00:53:46.490
to help us through that, to
try to make it as easy as

00:53:46.490 --> 00:53:49.940
possible for people to
deliver their work while also

00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:53.230
still doing it in
a secure manner.

00:53:53.230 --> 00:53:55.100
Yeah, I'm so glad
you brought that up.

00:53:55.100 --> 00:53:56.980
That is another one
of the key things

00:53:56.980 --> 00:53:58.840
that the Insight
Scientist Community

00:53:58.840 --> 00:54:04.270
does, is serve as a collective
voice for the technical people

00:54:04.270 --> 00:54:06.040
on the ground.

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:12.820
Because you have-- what you get
is you have senior leaders that

00:54:12.820 --> 00:54:15.340
go talk to tech
company executives

00:54:15.340 --> 00:54:18.580
and hear sales pitches and
whatever, and it's like,

00:54:18.580 --> 00:54:22.420
this is so easy, it'll
accelerate your work.

00:54:22.420 --> 00:54:26.290
And then they bring them
in and we do a training.

00:54:26.290 --> 00:54:30.100
And then it's like, OK, I'm
committed to this training,

00:54:30.100 --> 00:54:33.620
I see all the content, we're
working through it, it's good.

00:54:33.620 --> 00:54:36.430
And then we get it in
the Dow environment

00:54:36.430 --> 00:54:39.820
and with the Dow security
policies and work

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:42.970
processes and all that
stuff, and it's nothing

00:54:42.970 --> 00:54:45.250
like it was in the training.

00:54:45.250 --> 00:54:47.440
And then your
senior leaders don't

00:54:47.440 --> 00:54:51.670
understand why they haven't
seen the acceleration that they

00:54:51.670 --> 00:54:53.110
were told would happen.

00:54:53.110 --> 00:54:55.900
And it's just a
matter of education.

00:54:55.900 --> 00:55:00.430
It's not like we're saying
that security is wrong.

00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:02.860
They need to do what
they need to do,

00:55:02.860 --> 00:55:04.960
but everybody needs
to understand that

00:55:04.960 --> 00:55:08.800
that's happening and that it's
not just the out-of-the-box

00:55:08.800 --> 00:55:10.720
thing there.

00:55:10.720 --> 00:55:13.240
And so we can serve
as a collective voice

00:55:13.240 --> 00:55:16.120
for those shared
experiences around

00:55:16.120 --> 00:55:18.190
because the community
steering team does

00:55:18.190 --> 00:55:23.080
have access to the senior
leaders of each of the data

00:55:23.080 --> 00:55:24.560
science groups
around the function.

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:28.030
So there is a-- we did build
an intentional communication

00:55:28.030 --> 00:55:34.390
path to senior leadership from
the Insight Scientist Community

00:55:34.390 --> 00:55:36.700
leadership.

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:39.160
One thing I will
say again to answer,

00:55:39.160 --> 00:55:43.120
I guess, the specific
question is--

00:55:43.120 --> 00:55:47.960
so from those experiences--

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:52.108
and we also have
a lot of training

00:55:52.108 --> 00:55:54.400
we're doing for people who
are never going to be a data

00:55:54.400 --> 00:55:58.600
scientist, but they
either generate data

00:55:58.600 --> 00:56:03.370
or they might use data for
day-to-day decision-making

00:56:03.370 --> 00:56:07.480
within their jobs or
whatever, everybody

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:09.862
needs some kind of data
literacy at this point.

00:56:09.862 --> 00:56:12.070
And so we have those kinds
of training programs, too.

00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:14.260
And we have to be even more
sensitive to that issue

00:56:14.260 --> 00:56:17.740
probably because it's not
really in their job description.

00:56:17.740 --> 00:56:22.210
So we're making them do these
trainings that are possibly

00:56:22.210 --> 00:56:25.900
not something they think
of as their core role.

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:29.170
And then if they
don't use it, then we

00:56:29.170 --> 00:56:30.950
can really get those
resentment feelings.

00:56:30.950 --> 00:56:32.950
So what we've done
is just basically

00:56:32.950 --> 00:56:37.390
really, really
customize the training

00:56:37.390 --> 00:56:40.270
to Dow and to their role.

00:56:40.270 --> 00:56:42.490
So we don't deliver the
training unless it's

00:56:42.490 --> 00:56:45.650
got examples of literally,
this is what you do,

00:56:45.650 --> 00:56:48.220
you make these kinds of
spreadsheets or whatever,

00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:49.910
you need to do it in this way.

00:56:49.910 --> 00:56:54.070
And so yeah, I think
the issue you point out

00:56:54.070 --> 00:56:56.590
is definitely a real thing.

00:56:56.590 --> 00:57:03.250
And I guess the gist of creating
the content for that role

00:57:03.250 --> 00:57:05.510
is that if you can't
figure out how to do that,

00:57:05.510 --> 00:57:07.840
then maybe you should
reconsider if that training is

00:57:07.840 --> 00:57:09.970
needed for that role.

00:57:09.970 --> 00:57:14.200
So yeah, that's the
approach that we've

00:57:14.200 --> 00:57:19.140
taken with our general
literacy training.

00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:20.890
Really helpful points.

00:57:20.890 --> 00:57:23.490
Thank you so much for that.

00:57:23.490 --> 00:57:26.070
Maybe in the final
minutes here to tie it

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:29.940
back to what we had
opened up with about some

00:57:29.940 --> 00:57:32.670
of the conferences
and competitions,

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:34.650
I'm thinking about
people who have

00:57:34.650 --> 00:57:37.020
asked how to do these as well.

00:57:37.020 --> 00:57:42.330
How did you actually learn what
was needed for the conference

00:57:42.330 --> 00:57:47.485
or what goes into planning
an event like that?

00:57:47.485 --> 00:57:47.985
Yeah.

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:54.697
Luckily, because Dow is a huge
company, we do have people--

00:57:54.697 --> 00:57:56.530
like we haven't used
them actually for this,

00:57:56.530 --> 00:57:59.970
but there are people whose
entire job is planning events.

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:04.050
And so we have lots
of admin support.

00:58:04.050 --> 00:58:07.650
So people who can fill
out a whole bunch of forms

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:09.960
for badge access, who
can do a lot of stuff.

00:58:09.960 --> 00:58:13.350
So there's pretty
known things there

00:58:13.350 --> 00:58:18.630
on how do you get a bunch of
people into one Dow location.

00:58:18.630 --> 00:58:20.397
In terms of everything
else, I think

00:58:20.397 --> 00:58:22.230
we've learned a lot
from the poster sessions

00:58:22.230 --> 00:58:24.690
that we've done every
year since 2015.

00:58:24.690 --> 00:58:29.490
So that helps us learn how to
gather technical submissions,

00:58:29.490 --> 00:58:33.450
how to set up a
SharePoint to receive

00:58:33.450 --> 00:58:36.360
abstracts and things like that.

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:37.920
We even this year--

00:58:37.920 --> 00:58:43.890
people submitted their
proposal via a SharePoint list,

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:46.440
and then they added their
abstract and some extra

00:58:46.440 --> 00:58:47.950
information once
they were accepted,

00:58:47.950 --> 00:58:51.420
and then we used a
Power App to then make

00:58:51.420 --> 00:58:56.820
the program for the sessions
available on mobile.

00:58:56.820 --> 00:58:59.040
So we didn't have
to print programs.

00:58:59.040 --> 00:59:03.150
We could be green and just do
electronic programs and things.

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:06.840
So yeah, we have our
Microsoft experts

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:11.100
using all the full
capabilities of NetSuite.

00:59:11.100 --> 00:59:15.810
And yeah, the other key thing
is, you gotta schedule the day

00:59:15.810 --> 00:59:17.110
around the executives.

00:59:17.110 --> 00:59:23.550
So it's not easy to pick a
date, especially considering

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:25.230
it does have eight--

00:59:25.230 --> 00:59:27.180
six to eight weeks
of lead up to it.

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:31.440
So you have to have a time when
folks can be working on that

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:35.610
and you don't have the August
month of vacation for Europe

00:59:35.610 --> 00:59:39.360
and you don't have December off
for North America and that kind

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:40.030
of stuff.

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:42.150
But other than that,
you gotta schedule

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:47.670
it around the executives
mostly is what we've learned.

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:48.750
Yep.

00:59:48.750 --> 00:59:50.222
Yeah, so definitely
start small--

00:59:50.222 --> 00:59:52.180
ALIX SCHMIDT: --a year
to plan the whole thing.

00:59:52.180 --> 00:59:52.950
So, I mean, it's--

00:59:52.950 --> 00:59:53.783
AMANDA AHRENS: Yeah. --every

00:59:53.783 --> 00:59:55.920
other year because
it's a lot.

00:59:55.920 --> 00:59:56.788
A lot of work.

00:59:56.788 --> 00:59:57.580
ALIX SCHMIDT: Yeah.

00:59:57.580 --> 00:59:59.010
Yeah.

00:59:59.010 --> 01:00:01.650
Definitely start small
and literally keep

01:00:01.650 --> 01:00:02.940
every single document.

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:06.990
Like if you spent more than like
15 minutes crafting an email,

01:00:06.990 --> 01:00:11.930
make sure you save that so that
it can be reused in the future

01:00:11.930 --> 01:00:14.700
or at least referenced back to,
even if you're just starting

01:00:14.700 --> 01:00:17.100
off with something smaller
like a poster session,

01:00:17.100 --> 01:00:18.240
because then eventually--

01:00:18.240 --> 01:00:19.560
I mean, we just naturally grew.

01:00:19.560 --> 01:00:20.977
We're like, oh,
well what if we do a poster

01:00:20.977 --> 01:00:22.890
session and some presentations

01:00:22.890 --> 01:00:26.580
or a poster session and
the Data Science Challenge?

01:00:26.580 --> 01:00:31.370
And then it turned into
a large conference.

01:00:31.370 --> 01:00:33.290
And don't forget to have fun.

01:00:33.290 --> 01:00:37.940
Like we wanted to-- we scheduled
in certain times for fun

01:00:37.940 --> 01:00:40.500
and just hanging out and
catching up with people,

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:41.540
especially post-COVID.

01:00:41.540 --> 01:00:43.557
I mean, that was
really important.

01:00:43.557 --> 01:00:44.390
AMANDA AHRENS: Yeah.

01:00:44.390 --> 01:00:46.240
Absolutely.

01:00:46.240 --> 01:00:49.188
AMANDA AHRENS: We'll celebrate
with mimosas or drinks after.

01:00:49.188 --> 01:00:49.980
ALIX SCHMIDT: Yeah.

01:00:49.980 --> 01:00:51.563
AMANDA AHRENS: The
Organization Team--

01:00:51.563 --> 01:00:54.800
Planning Team advice, yeah.

01:00:54.800 --> 01:00:56.050
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's great.

01:00:56.050 --> 01:00:59.680
Well, thank you both so much for
joining us here and answering

01:00:59.680 --> 01:01:00.980
everybody's questions.

01:01:00.980 --> 01:01:04.450
Thank you all for the
great questions, too.

01:01:04.450 --> 01:01:05.350
I appreciate it.

01:01:05.350 --> 01:01:09.990
And hope to see everybody
back next Thursday, too.

